# Shell scripts must keep LF endings. With core.autocrlf=true on Windows a
# fresh clone would otherwise give them CRLF, and bash then reads the trailing
# \r as part of each command — every line fails with "command not found".
*.sh text eol=lf

# The specification documents are edited on Windows; leave their endings to git's
# normal handling so diffs stay clean.
*.md text

# The hash lock is read by `sha256sum -c`, which does not tolerate CR in its
# input. A CRLF checkout would make rule 4 fail on every machine but this one.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
